    <!-- ===================================================
         IDLE SELF-SUSTAIN PROBLEM + DYNAMO SOLUTION
         =================================================== -->
    <hr class="p11-sep" />
    <div class="p11-section-label">The Idle Problem — and the Dynamo Solution</div>
    <h2 class="p11-section-title">Why Phase 10 could not self-sustain at idle — and how Phase 11 fixes it</h2>
    <p class="p11-section-lead">
        At idle (20 PSI), the two pistons push the rails slowly. The static engine's internal rotors turn at low RPM.
        The generator, even in low gear, cannot produce the 4,200W the boiler heater demands at such low shaft speed.
        Phase 10 acknowledged this gap (−3,560W deficit at idle) and relied on battery bridge time.
        Phase 11 introduces the Dynamo — a small DC unit mechanically coupled to the flywheel or
        hand-crank started — to inject direct current into the battery bus during this gap,
        reducing or eliminating battery drain at idle until the generator comes up to self-sustain RPM.
    </p>

    <div class="p11-dynamo-concepts">
        <div class="p11-dynamo-card">
            <div class="p11-dynamo-badge">Concept A</div>
            <h3>Hand-Crank Dynamo Start</h3>
            <p>A hand-crank on the dynamo shaft allows an operator to spin up the dynamo to ~500 RPM in 10–15 seconds, producing a burst of 24V DC that pre-charges the supercap and begins warming the battery bus. This bypasses the cold-start problem without any external power source. Once the pistons begin moving and the generator comes online, the dynamo decouples automatically via a one-way bearing (similar to an automotive alternator).</p>
            <div class="p11-dynamo-stat"><span>Start energy needed</span><span>~5 kJ (hand-crank achievable)</span></div>
            <div class="p11-dynamo-stat"><span>Dynamo output burst</span><span>~960W / 40 seconds</span></div>
            <div class="p11-dynamo-stat"><span>Decouples at</span><span>Generator &gt; 60% rated RPM</span></div>
        </div>
        <div class="p11-dynamo-card">
            <div class="p11-dynamo-badge p11-badge-b">Concept B</div>
            <h3>Permanent Magnet Flywheel Dynamo</h3>
            <p>Permanent magnets embedded in the flywheel rim pass a fixed stator coil as the flywheel spins. Because the flywheel never fully stops (even at idle it retains angular momentum), this approach produces a small but continuous DC trickle — estimated 200–400W at idle flywheel speed. This trickle reduces the net battery drain from −3,560W to approximately −3,160 to −3,360W, extending idle battery bridge time significantly. No moving parts beyond what already exists.</p>
            <div class="p11-dynamo-stat"><span>Idle output estimate</span><span>200–400W continuous</span></div>
            <div class="p11-dynamo-stat"><span>Added complexity</span><span>Low — magnets in flywheel rim</span></div>
            <div class="p11-dynamo-stat"><span>Best combined with</span><span>Concept A (warm start)</span></div>
        </div>
        <div class="p11-dynamo-card">
            <div class="p11-dynamo-badge p11-badge-c">Concept C</div>
            <h3>Supercap Pre-Charge Kick</h3>
            <p>A dedicated 500F supercapacitor bank, pre-charged when the system is parked, delivers a high-current burst to the dynamo field winding at start. This magnetises the rotor instantly and allows the dynamo to produce rated output from the first rotation — no hand-cranking needed. Once the generator is online, the supercap recharges passively from the generator output. Requires the supercap to retain charge between sessions (they self-discharge over days — acceptable for daily use).</p>
            <div class="p11-dynamo-stat"><span>Supercap retention</span><span>~80% after 24hr at rest</span></div>
            <div class="p11-dynamo-stat"><span>Kick duration</span><span>~3–8 seconds to dynamo rated speed</span></div>
            <div class="p11-dynamo-stat"><span>No human input needed</span><span>Fully automatic start</span></div>
        </div>
    </div>

