                    <span>−1,780 W ↑ from −3,560</span>
                </div>
            </div>
            <div class="p11-budget-note">
                Dynamo assist cuts the idle battery drain by <strong style="color:#FFD700">~50%</strong>.
                At 40kWh battery capacity, idle bridge time extends from ~10 hours to <strong style="color:#39FF14">~22 hours</strong>.
                For a vehicle rarely idling for more than 30 minutes, this is effectively self-sustaining for practical use.
            </div>
        </div>

        <div class="p11-budget-panel">
            <h3 class="p11-budget-title">Crossover Point — When does the Generator take over?</h3>
            <div class="p11-crossover">
                <div class="p11-cross-item">
                    <div class="p11-cross-val" id="p11-xover-psi">~60 PSI</div>
                    <div class="p11-cross-lbl">Steam pressure at crossover</div>
                </div>
                <div class="p11-cross-item">
                    <div class="p11-cross-val" id="p11-xover-rpm">~185 RPM</div>
                    <div class="p11-cross-lbl">Internal rotor RPM at crossover</div>
                </div>
                <div class="p11-cross-item p11-cross-green">
                    <div class="p11-cross-val">~4,320 W</div>
                    <div class="p11-cross-lbl">Generator output at crossover</div>
                </div>
                <div class="p11-cross-item">
                    <div class="p11-cross-val">~35 sec</div>
                    <div class="p11-cross-lbl">Warm-up time (cold start)</div>
                </div>
            </div>
            <p class="p11-cross-desc">
                Once rail pressure exceeds ~60 PSI, the static engine's internal rotors reach ~185 RPM.
                In low gear, the generator produces enough output to exceed the 4,200W heater demand.
                The PCW controller detects this threshold and automatically disconnects the dynamo field
                excitation, letting the generator carry the full load. The dynamo idles freely on its
                one-way bearing — no drag on the system.
            </p>
            <div class="p11-cross-callout">
                <strong>PCW Crossover Logic:</strong>
                <code>if (gen_output_W &gt;= heater_demand_W) → disengage_dynamo_field();</code>
                <span>100Hz poll · &lt;10ms switching time</span>
            </div>
        </div>
    </div>

    <!-- ===================================================
         GENERATOR LOW / HIGH GEAR CONCEPT
         =================================================== -->
    <hr class="p11-sep" />
    <div class="p11-section-label">Generator Gear Selector — New in Phase 11</div>
    <h2 class="p11-section-title">Low gear at idle · High gear at cruise — matching generator RPM to rail speed</h2>

    <div class="p11-gear-grid">
        <div class="p11-gear-card p11-gear-low">
            <div class="p11-gear-badge">LOW GEAR</div>
            <h3>Idle → ~90 PSI</h3>
            <p>Low gear increases the step-up ratio from the internal rotor to the generator shaft. At slow rotor speeds (idle, light throttle), low gear multiplies RPM to keep the generator in its efficient operating band. The generator produces usable output even when the static engine turns slowly. Trade-off: lower torque multiplication means the generator shaft sees less force — suitable when steam pressure is low.</p>
            <div class="p11-gear-stat"><span>Rotor RPM range</span><span>50 – 200 RPM</span></div>
            <div class="p11-gear-stat"><span>Gen shaft RPM (×4 ratio)</span><span>200 – 800 RPM</span></div>
            <div class="p11-gear-stat"><span>Estimated output</span><span>400 – 4,500 W</span></div>
        </div>
        <div class="p11-gear-card p11-gear-high">
            <div class="p11-gear-badge p11-badge-high">HIGH GEAR</div>
            <h3>~90 PSI → Full throttle</h3>
            <p>High gear reduces the step-up ratio, allowing the generator to handle higher torque input without over-speeding. At cruise and full throttle, the static engine's internal rotors turn fast enough that high gear keeps the generator in its rated RPM band without damage. This is where surplus production happens — high gear + high rotor speed = maximum kilowatt output with safe thermal loading on the generator windings.</p>
            <div class="p11-gear-stat"><span>Rotor RPM range</span><span>200 – 600 RPM</span></div>
            <div class="p11-gear-stat"><span>Gen shaft RPM (×2 ratio)</span><span>400 – 1,200 RPM</span></div>
            <div class="p11-gear-stat"><span>Estimated output</span><span>4,500 – 9,600 W</span></div>
        </div>
        <div class="p11-gear-card p11-gear-auto">
            <div class="p11-gear-badge p11-badge-auto">AUTO (PCW)</div>
            <h3>PCW-controlled crossover</h3>
