            <strong>Phase 11 Concept Note — For Phase 12 Refinement</strong>
            <p>
                The hardest part of this design to put into words is correct as of Phase 11:
                the engine is a static sealed oil-bath unit — more like a static transmission than a reciprocating engine.
                Its internal rotating members (gears, shafts, bearings) are set in motion by the two steam-driven rails and the three wheels on each side.
                The only reciprocating action in the system happens at the two rear pistons near the boiler.
                The custom generator sits on top and takes its input from the internal rotor's output shaft.
                Phase 12 should focus on: (1) the physical geometry of how rail linear motion converts to rotary inside the static engine, (2) which of the three dynamo concepts (A, B, or C) is most practical to bench-test first, and (3) whether a physical mock-up of the generator-on-engine coupling can be built to validate gear ratios before committing to machined parts.
                All calculations in this phase are theoretical — real-world output will vary with bearing friction, oil viscosity, steam condensation losses, and gear mesh efficiency.
